Decide whether SELinux is in force from this process's label.

is_selinux_enabled() needs selinuxfs mounted and /etc/selinux/config
present. A container's mount namespace has neither, so a criu run in one
concludes there is no LSM: it dumps no labels, and what it restores keeps
criu's own label instead of the container's.

diff --git a/criu/lsm.c b/criu/lsm.c
index 0ea2957..9bda04c 100644
--- a/criu/lsm.c
+++ b/criu/lsm.c
@@ -236,13 +236,13 @@ void kerndat_lsm(void)
 	}
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_HAS_SELINUX
-	if (is_selinux_enabled()) {
+	{
 		char *ctx;
 
 		/*
-		 * CRIU used to only check if /sys/fs/selinux is mounted, but that does not
-		 * seem to be enough for CRIU's use case. CRIU actually needs to look if
-		 * a valid label is returned.
+		 * is_selinux_enabled() wants selinuxfs mounted and /etc/selinux/config
+		 * present, and a container's mount namespace has neither. The label
+		 * the kernel reports for this process does not depend on them.
 		 */
 		if (getpidcon_raw(getpid(), &ctx) < 0)
 			goto no_lsm;
